>=2)+ theme_void()+ theme(legend.position

>="none")+xlim(c(1,3))+ ylim(c(0,3))

>
>My problem: the points of the arrow overlap a bit.

I think this is inevitable with line segments. Any points on the line are,
I think, in the middle of the line. So if two lines end at the same point
the lines will overlap slightly.

>My question: how do I get them to crisply touch at just (vanishingly
>small, non-rounded) tippy tips?

I don't really know the answer, but my guess is that you might need to
adjust the point position to account for the line width or use narrow
polygons (with a fill but no colour) rather than lines. All a bit fiddly.
I'd go with polygons.

>Bonus question: Ideally, the lines would end in a 90 degree chevron
>points, meeting at right angles, with no arrow edges jutting out (sample
>attached image). Is this possible? My ultimate goal is a pdf/svg type
>image that resolves identically regardless
> of size.

For your small example how does this look:

# Expanded data specifying segments as polygons
# with a chevron at the meeting point:
DF<-data.frame(x=c(1,1.9,2,1.9,1,3,2.1,2,2.1,3,1.9,1.9,2.1,2.1,2),
               y=c(1.1,1.1,1,0.9,0.9,1.1,1.1,1,0.9,0.9,1.1,2,2,1.1,1),
               colours=rep(c("yellow","blue","lightgreen"),each=5),
               stringsAsFactors=FALSE)

ggplot(DF)+
 geom_polygon(aes(x=x,y=y,group=colours,fill=colours))+
 coord_fixed()+
 theme_void()+
 scale_fill_manual(values=c("yellow","blue","lightgreen"))+
 theme(legend.position="none")

If you needed to do it frequently you could come up with a function that
switched the segment data to polygon data for a given width.

Hopefully someone else with come up with a (presumably simpler)
geom_segment based solution for you.

Ron.

